# Personal worklog (auto-maintained, never committed)

Keep a private, always-current record of what *you* are working on, so anyone — you, or the next agent — sees the live state at a glance without digging through chat history or the tracker. This log is **personal and local**: a companion to the tracker and the task lifecycle, never a replacement for either.

## Where it lives

- One file per day, named `YYYY-MM-DD.md`, under the target repo's `AGENTS.md` → key `Worklog path` (create the folder if the repo lacks it).
- That folder is **gitignored** — it must never be committed or show up in `git status`.

## When to update — three moments, ticket-independent

For any piece of work, with or without a ticket, update the day's file when:

1. **Work starts** → add an entry to the **active** section.
2. **Status changes** → update that entry's status and/or its `→` next action.
3. **Work finishes** → move the entry out of the active section into the **done** section.

The user may also edit the file by hand at any time; never discard their manual entries.

## Marking work done

- **Ticket-bound work:** on merge / close-out, move it to the done section with `✅`.
- **Ad-hoc work (no ticket):** move it to the done section but mark it `❓` until the user confirms — only then switch to `✅`. Never mark ad-hoc work finally done on your own.

## Day rollover

On the first write of a new day, create that day's file and **carry over the still-open entries** from the most recent previous file. Do not carry over done items.

## Section headings and status labels

Both are **values**, because both are prose in the document's language (the target repo's `AGENTS.md` → key `Doc language`):

- the two headings — the target repo's `AGENTS.md` → key `Worklog section headings`
- the status labels — the target repo's `AGENTS.md` → key `Worklog status labels`

What is **not** configurable is the state set and its emoji. The emoji is the state's machine-readable identity and is the same in every language; the label next to it is only how a human reads it:

| Emoji | State | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| 🔄 | in progress | actively being worked on |
| ⛔ | blocked | stopped by an obstacle |
| ⏳ | waiting | waiting on something or someone (test, review, approval) |
| 👀 | in review | in review / PR review |
| 🧪 | implemented | code done, not yet closed out (e.g. awaiting a test) |
| ⏸️ | parked | deliberately paused |

`implemented` uses `🧪`, not `✅`, on purpose: `✅` marks a **done** entry, and the two must never be confusable.

### Reading: recognise the stock

The headings are language-dependent and **the existing files are never migrated**, so both forms exist permanently. When reading a worklog — especially on day rollover, where missing the previous day's open entries loses them silently — recognise **all** of these:

- `## Aktiv` / `## Erledigt` (German)
- `## Active` / `## Done` (English)

Match the emoji, not the label, when you need to identify a state programmatically.

## Entry format

Keep the log **scannable at a glance** — especially the active section. Every entry is one short line, never a multi-clause note.

**Active entry** — three mandatory parts, in this order:

1. **Short title** — enough to recall what it is about.
2. **Status** — exactly one emoji plus its label.
3. **Next action / wait** — short free text prefixed with `→`: what to do next, or what you are waiting for.

Optionally between status and next action: a **ticket reference** `· <key(s)>` — just the key(s), e.g. `· ANW-7660` or `· ANW-7660 / AURA-664`, never prose.

Nothing else belongs in an active entry: no worktree, container or branch names, no test counts, no detailed tracker status. Those live in the ticket.

**Done entry** — title + `✅` + optional key(s). The close-out detail (test result, merge info, final status) belongs in the ticket's close-out comment, not here.

```markdown
# Worklog YYYY-MM-DD

## <active heading>
- <short title> — <emoji> <label> [· <key(s)>] → <next action / wait>

## <done heading>
- <short title> — ✅ [· <key(s)>]
```

## ✅ / ❌

- ✅ Start a piece of work → add it to the active section; on finish move it to the done section with `✅`.
- ✅ One scannable line: `- Worklog kürzen — 🔄 in-arbeit · ANW-7660 → offene Skill-Stellen anpassen`.
- ✅ Exactly one state, as emoji + label: `🧪 implementiert → wartet auf Test`.
- ❌ Free-form multi-clause status: `🔄 Welle 2 fertig, wartet auf Test · Worktree anw7593 · volle Suite grün`.
- ❌ Worktree, container, branch or test detail in the line instead of in the ticket.
- ❌ Committing the worklog folder, or leaving it out of `.gitignore`.
- ❌ Marking ad-hoc work `✅` before the user confirms (use `❓` first).
- ❌ The same item listed in both sections.
- ❌ Creating a second active section because the existing one used the other language's heading.

## Verification checklist

- [ ] The current day's file exists under the configured worklog path and reflects reality.
- [ ] Each active entry is one scannable line: title + one state (emoji + label) + a `→` next action; ticket reference optional; nothing else.
- [ ] Done entries are title + `✅` + optional key; close-out detail lives in the ticket comment.
- [ ] The previous file was read under **both** heading spellings, so no open entry was lost on rollover.
- [ ] `git status` never shows the worklog folder.
- [ ] Ad-hoc "done" waits for user confirmation.
